d independent poll confirming the September survey, or further national movement toward Democrats, would push this race into tossup territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Brad Finstad (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Minnesota's 1st District leans Republican by about six points, and incumbent Brad Finstad enters the fall with the advantages of office after easily winning renomination in August. Most professional ratings place the race in the Likely Republican column, though two outlets rate it Lean Republican after Cook Political Report downgraded Finstad's odds in August.\n\nDemocrat Jake Johnson has kept the race competitive. He has raised nearly as much money as Finstad, and a late-September poll from a Democratic-affiliated firm showed him ahead by a point. Earlier spring surveys gave Finstad leads of three to ten points, and prediction markets currently price Johnson's chances at roughly one in four. The balance of evidence points to a modest Republican edge, with enough uncertainty that a strong Democratic year could make this genuinely competitive.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1904, "margin": -5.38}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.19, "margin": -6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1925, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.3207, "margin": -3.5}]}